## <div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Volunteering in Our Community – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Supporting Our Community</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Volunteering in Our Community</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Explain how to organize projects or events. Interactive: Facilitate group decision-making on project planning.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can explain the steps involved in how the government or local authorities support a community event (e.g., "To organize a community event, you should first plan the route. Then, you should apply for permits and coordinate with local government officials for road closures and safety. Finally, you should collaborate with community outreach departments to promote the event.")<br>Can facilitate group discussions to decide on key elements for planning a community volunteer project (e.g., "To plan the project, we should choose a cause we care about, select a date that works for everyone, and decide how to divide tasks, such as gathering supplies, creating signs, or reaching out to local organizations.")<br>Can respond to group questions during planning sessions (e.g., "We have been discussing what resources are needed for the event, and it seems we still need volunteers and supplies.")</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 55%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 10%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of the lesson's target language and topic.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a series of images showing different community volunteer projects (e.g., beach clean-up, food drive, building a shelter, tree planting). Students, in pairs, briefly discuss which project they would most like to participate in and why. They should mention one responsibility they would be willing to take. (e.g., "I would like to participate in the clean-up because it is a very important initiative. I could help collect trash." or "We should clean up our local park to make it a more positive area for the community.")</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models sentences explaining community support and planning, using the passive voice, modals for necessity, and present perfect continuous. For example: "To organize a successful clean-up, volunteers should first identify a high-risk area. Then, resources have been provided by the local government for supplies. Finally, participants are always needed." The teacher stresses content words (e.g., VOL-un-TEERS, ID-en-TI-fy, RE-sources, GOV-ern-ment) and practices alternating between stressed and unstressed syllables for fluency. The teacher asks students to repeat key phrases, focusing on rhythm and stress.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a project 'is funded by the government,' who provides the money?" (The government provides the money.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does 'volunteers should contribute' mean? Is it a suggestion or a strong recommendation?" (It means it's a strong recommendation or obligation.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to the Scenario: "Supporting Our Community."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short audio dialogue (2-3 minutes) between two community organizers discussing the steps for planning a local "Community Park Beautification Project." The dialogue incorporates phrases like: "The permits have been applied for," "Funds are being allocated by the local council," "We should involve more diverse participants," "Volunteers have been working tirelessly on outreach."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Explore how different communities express and value support needs through language and communication.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Speaking focus):</b> After listening, students respond orally to questions in pairs, then share with the class:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What main steps are being discussed for organizing the park beautification project?" (Students should identify steps like applying for permits, allocating funds, and involving volunteers.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Who has been contributing most to the initial planning, according to the dialogue?" (The local council and existing volunteers.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Why is involving diverse participants seen as important for the project's impact?" (To bring different perspectives and ensure broader community involvement.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking through controlled and semi-controlled activities.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice using the passive voice (e.g., "Community programs are funded by the government."), modals for necessity (e.g., "Individuals should contribute to community projects."), and present perfect continuous (e.g., "Volunteers have been working to improve community services.") with target vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Guided Sentence Completion (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher provides sentence stems on the board or screen, focused on community support and planning. Students complete them orally in pairs, then share with the class.</li>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: circle; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"For our volunteer project, funding _____ (provide) by local businesses." (Expected: "is provided")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"To ensure success, volunteers _____ (should / dedicate) time for planning." (Expected: "should dedicate")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The team _____ (work) on promoting the event since last week." (Expected: "has been working")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Key decisions _____ (make) in our last meeting." (Expected: "were made" or "have been made")</li>
                    </ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Picture-Prompted Description (Semi-Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher shows images of various community issues (e.g., a dirty park, an underserved community, an empty shelter). In small groups, students discuss and explain what "should be done" or "has been done" to address the issue, using target grammar and vocabulary. Each group shares one solution orally.</li>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: circle; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example image: A dirty park. Student response: "The park should be cleaned up by a volunteer brigade. Funds could be allocated by the local government for equipment. Community involvement has been crucial for previous clean-up initiatives."</li>
                    </ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Collaborative Mini-Dialogue (Pair Interaction):</b> Students work in pairs to create a short dialogue (4-6 exchanges) where one student asks about organizing a small community initiative (e.g., a neighborhood clean-up, a recycling drive), and the other explains the steps, using passive voice, modals, and present perfect continuous. They then switch roles. Focus on stress patterns and rhythm.</li>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: circle; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "How can we organize a new volunteer brigade for the recycling initiative?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "First, responsibilities should be assigned. Then, a meeting must be called to discuss resources. We have been collecting ideas from the community, which is helpful."</li>
                    </ul>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students use the language in a realistic scenario: "Supporting Our Community."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group Simulation / Role-Play: "Community Project Planning Meeting."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> Students are divided into groups of 3-4. Each group acts as a "Community Initiatives Committee" tasked with planning a new volunteer project for their local area. They choose one community issue from a provided list (e.g., supporting an animal shelter, providing services to an underserved area, developing a youth sports program). Their goal is to have a discussion to:
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Decide on the project's purpose and specific goals.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Outline the steps involved in organizing the project, explaining how governmental or local authorities might support it (using passive voice).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Discuss the roles and responsibilities of volunteers (using modals for necessity).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Reference any ongoing efforts or challenges they have been facing (using present perfect continuous).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students must lead collaborative discussions, offer diverse solutions, and respond to questions from their group members. Teacher circulates, observing fluency, collaborative interaction, and appropriate use of target language.</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Example phrases students might use:</b> "To organize this initiative, permits should be secured from the municipality." "We have been assessing the needs of the underserved families in that area." "Community involvement is crucial, so volunteers must be engaged early on." "How has the local government been encouraging more people to participate?" "Why has community involvement been important for everyone involved?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment and student self-reflection on learning outcomes.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist. The teacher observes groups during the "Community Project Planning Meeting" (Stage 4), noting students' ability to explain project organization, facilitate decision-making, and use target grammar and vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Groups present a brief (1-minute) summary of their planned project and the key decisions they made, explaining the process and roles. Each member must speak.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation and clear articulation of words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (e.g., <i>resources, volunteers, initiative, responsibilities</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate use of passive voice, modals for necessity, and present perfect continuous.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to explain steps for project organization and facilitate group decision-making.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active and collaborative participation in group discussions.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a brief exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Name one new word or phrase you used effectively today to explain project organization."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one challenge you faced in facilitating group decisions, and how did you try to overcome it?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Opportunities for student and teacher to reflect on the learning process and plan for future lessons.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do I feel now explaining how to organize a community event, and what specific language structures helped me the most?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most impactful part of participating in the group planning, and how did my contribution help the team?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this speaking lesson? (e.g., "The group discussion activity successfully encouraged students to use the target grammar and vocabulary spontaneously. Pronunciation practice with stressed syllables in stage 3 improved fluency.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for the next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition)? (e.g., "Some students still struggled with the correct form of the passive voice in real-time conversation; a quick review or visual aid may be needed before the writing task. For Lesson 4, students can write a formal proposal based on their discussion, ensuring they apply the same structures accurately in written form.")</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 4, students will use the ideas generated and language practiced today to write a formal proposal or a report about their planned community volunteer project, further consolidating their understanding and application of the theme and target language.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>